SCOPE - WHAT CC BY DOES AND DOES NOT COVER

This matters, so it is stated precisely rather than left to inference.

NOT COVERED. The federal government works these datasets are derived FROM are in
the public domain under 17 U.S.C. 105 and no rights are asserted over them: the
Department of State Visa Bulletin, DOL PERM disclosure files, USCIS
Administrative Appeals Office decisions, USCIS inventory data, the Federal
Register, and the eCFR.

ALSO NOT COVERED. Any individual fact extracted from those works - an outcome, a
date, a docket number, a cutoff, a quoted span of the agency's own words - is
likewise public domain. Facts are not copyrightable, and this project does not
purport to make them so by putting them in a table. If you want the underlying
facts and nothing else, take them freely and without attribution.

COVERED. What CC BY attaches to is the original expression this project added:
the selection of fields, the schema and its documentation, the classification
choices, and the written prose in the data dictionary, methodology note, and
site pages.

WHY SAY IT THIS WAY. Under Feist Publications v. Rural Telephone Service, 499
U.S. 340 (1991), the United States has no "sweat of the brow" database right -
compilation copyright is thin and reaches only original selection and
arrangement. Asserting more than that over public records is a real problem
(sometimes called copyfraud), and it would also be self-defeating: the point of
this dataset is that people use it. So the attribution condition is deliberately
narrow. It asks for credit for the work of building the thing, not for the
public records inside it.

PRACTICAL EFFECT FOR REUSERS

  - Reusing the dataset as published, or its documentation: attribute.
  - Extracting facts and rebuilding your own structure: no obligation.
  - Ingesting the source documents directly from the agency: no obligation, and
    this license is irrelevant to you.

That last case is the normal one for public-access projects such as the Free Law
Project, which collect from the originating agency rather than from a
third-party dataset. Nothing here constrains that.

AUDITABILITY

Because every parsed field records the span of source text it was extracted
from, any row can be audited against the original government document. If you
find a discrepancy, the source document governs, not this dataset.

